    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "where C is the covariance matrix between u* and u, and G is the covariance matrix associated with u (Henderson 1977b). This is just the linear regression of u* on u (see Equation 8.27). Note that the vectors u* and u can have different dimensionality, so that if u* is $ r \\times 1 $ and u is $ q \\times 1 $, then C is an $ r \\times q $ matrix with $ C_{ij} = \\sigma(u_{*}i, u_j) $.",
